# Next.js 16.2.3 — what changed vs your training data

This project uses **Next.js 16.2.3, React 19.2.4, Webpack** (`npm run dev` → `next dev --webpack`). Bundled `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` is **NOT installed** in this version — do not try to read it.

## Async APIs (this is the big one)

`cookies()`, `headers()`, `draftMode()`, `params`, and `searchParams` are all **Promises** in 16.

```ts
// Server component / page
export default async function Page({
  params,
  searchParams,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>;
  searchParams: Promise<Record<string, string | string[] | undefined>>;
}) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const sp = await searchParams;
}

// Route handler
export async function GET(
  req: Request,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> },
) {
  const { id } = await params;
}

// Anywhere
import { cookies, headers } from "next/headers";
const c = await cookies();
const h = await headers();
```

## Caching defaults (changed)

- Route handlers default to **dynamic** (no static optimization) when they read request data. Be explicit:
  ```ts
  export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"; // or "force-static"
  export const revalidate = 60;            // ISR
  export const runtime = "nodejs";         // or "edge"
  ```
- `fetch()` cache default is **no-store** for any request that depends on dynamic APIs. Set `cache: "force-cache"` or `next: { revalidate }` explicitly when you want caching.

## Config (`next.config.mjs`)

- The `eslint` config key was **removed** in 16. Lint runs separately (`eslint .` in CI).
- Top-level `env` block is gone in this project — anything declared there ships to the client bundle. Read server secrets via `process.env` in server code only.
- `serverExternalPackages` is the supported way to externalise native deps (we do this for `@prisma/client`, `@prisma/adapter-pg`, `pg`).
- Use `redirects()` / `rewrites()` async functions (we use `redirects()` for `/app/watchlist` → `/app/deals`).
- `typescript.ignoreBuildErrors` is `false` and stays that way.

## Middleware (`src/middleware.ts`)

- Runs on the Edge runtime by default. Don't import Node-only packages (Prisma, Stripe SDK).
- Use it for Supabase session refresh and route gating only. See `src/lib/supabase/middleware.ts` for the pattern.

## Routing & layouts

- App Router only. No `pages/` directory.
- Route groups: `(marketing)`, `(public)`, `app/`. Each can have its own `layout.tsx`.
- Dynamic catch-all: `[indexnowKey]` exists at the root for the IndexNow proof file.
- Special files: `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, `route.ts`, `layout.tsx`, `page.tsx`, `template.tsx`.

## Server vs client

- **Server is the default.** Add `"use client"` ONLY when you need state, effects, or browser APIs. Server components can pass server-fetched data into client components as props.
- Server actions (`"use server"`) are allowed but we mostly use REST handlers under `/api/*` for consistency. Don't introduce server actions for new features without checking with the user first.

## Common pitfalls in 16

- Forgetting to `await params` → TypeError "params is a Promise".
- `fetch()` from a server component without `cache` set → unintentionally dynamic on every render.
- Importing `@prisma/client` (the package) instead of `@/generated/prisma/client` (the generated client).
- Pinning Stripe `apiVersion` — see `architecture.mdc`.

## Reference

When unsure about a 16-specific behaviour, **search the web with year `2026`** (Next 16 was released in 2025, latest stable is 16.2.3). Don't guess from training data — App Router APIs have shifted in every minor since 14.
